  <!-- STEP 04 — THE VOICE PIPELINE -->
  <Section tone="paper" padding="default" id="pipeline" class="scroll-mt-24 border-t border-line">
    <Container width="content">
      <div class="case-layout">
        <div class="case-layout__head">
          <p class="mono-label mb-3">Step 04</p>
          <Heading level={2} visual="display-md" tone="ink">The voice pipeline, and where the model goes</Heading>
        </div>
        <div class="case-layout__body">
          <div class="mt-8 measure space-y-5 leading-relaxed text-ink-muted text-lg">
            <p>
              This is where design and engineering stopped being separable, and the reason
              the role was product design engineering rather than either half. A custom
              Picovoice Porcupine model runs in WebAssembly in the browser listening for
              &ldquo;log a tot,&rdquo; so
              <strong class="text-ink font-semibold">no audio leaves the device to detect
              the wake word</strong>. In a product whose microphone is live in a room full
              of other people&rsquo;s children, that is not a performance optimisation. It
              is the difference between an assistant and a surveillance device, and it is
              the answer to the first question every provider asked.
            </p>
            <p>
              Parsing is a <strong class="text-ink font-semibold">deterministic rules
              engine, not a language model</strong>. The transcript runs through an ordered
              ladder of 22 phrase matchers, and child names are matched against the roster
              plus each child&rsquo;s phonetic alias list, so one utterance can fan out to
              several children. A language model in the parse path would be slower,
              non-deterministic, and worst of all confidently wrong: a hallucinated category
              writes a nap that never happened into a record a parent reads and a licensor
              may audit. The rules engine either matches or does not, and what it does not
              match goes to a human queue with the audio attached.
              <code>unknown</code> is a first-class outcome, not a failure mode.
            </p>
            <p>
              The language model sits <em>ahead</em> of the problem instead. When a child is
              added, a background job asks an LLM for all variations, pronunciations, and
              likely mishearings of that name, and stores them as phonetic aliases that feed
              the matcher. Recognition failure on toddler names, which are short, similar,
              and frequently non-English, is the product&rsquo;s binding constraint. Rather
              than correct mistakes after they happen, the system pre-computes the ways a
              name is likely to be misheard and teaches the matcher to accept them. Human
              corrections in the repair queue append to the same field, so the vocabulary
              improves from both directions.
            </p>
            <p>
              The rest is shape and latency. Spanish is translated <em>before</em> matching
              rather than parsed separately, so one phrase library serves two languages
              instead of a second parser drifting out of sync. A single-child utterance
              processes inline so the row fills immediately; a multi-child one goes to a
              background job; audio uploads in a separate second request so the critical
              path carries a short string rather than a media blob. The row then updates
              over Pusher into a placeholder slot that was already rendered.
            </p>
          </div>

          {/* The pipeline this step describes, drawn end to end. Given the full
              column rather than a half, because the failure branch is the point. */}
