              On the interface side, the decisions that held up best were structural. The base
              focus style is inherited by the base button style, so every button in the
              application gets a visible focus ring
              <strong class="text-ink font-semibold">by construction rather than by
              remembering</strong>. The modal restores focus on close to the element that
              opened it. Voice status sits in an assertive live region, so the state a sighted
              user reads from a pulsing dot reaches a screen reader user too, and the pulse
              itself is wrapped in <code>motion-safe:</code>. The calendar differentiates event
              types with solid, dashed, and dotted bars rather than colour, which survives a
              month sheet printed in black and white and taped to the fridge. And
              <code>dark:print:text-black</code> means a provider printing a compliance report
              from a dark screen still gets black on white, which is exactly the class of bug
              that never appears in design review.
            </p>
            <p>
              Localisation goes deeper than string files. English and Spanish sit at parity,
              roughly 1,220 lines each, covering marketing, app chrome, enums, and error
              messages, with resolution running user preference to URL to session to
              <code>Accept-Language</code>. Date formatting branches on language, spoken
              announcements translate per listener, and the voice pipeline translates before
              matching. A meaningful share of providers in this market work primarily in
              Spanish, and a half-translated interface is arguably worse than an untranslated
              one.
